  "id": "Compound::DB00790 - Disease::DOID:3393",
  "verbalization": "Perindopril, an antihypertensive agent, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Coronary Artery Disease through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Perindopril causes Serositis, which is further mediated by Timolol or Enalapril, both of which are Ace Inhibitors or dicarboxylic acid monoesters. These agents, in turn, are linked to Coronary Artery Disease, indicating a potential role in the development of the condition. The shared lowest common ancestors—Antihypertensive Agent and Organic Heterocyclic Compound—highlight a common biological context, suggesting that Perindopril may influence vascular or inflammatory processes through these pathways. The integration of these mechanisms implies that Perindopril could contribute to Coronary Artery Disease via indirect effects on inflammation or vascular function, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
